      "text": "I want to be fully transparent with you here before going any further.\n\n---\n\n## Here's What I Was Able to Retrieve — and What I Can't Provide\n\n### What the tool returned (the opening passage)\n\nThe excerpt tool was only able to surface the same opening passage across all queries. Here is what it retrieved — and it is genuinely useful for your review:\n\n> *\"Alex drove. Alex always drove. I did the playlists and the snacks and the terrible jokes. Somewhere in a gas-station parking lot in western North Carolina, at three in the morning, I discovered that he had been laughing politely at me for six hours and finally, finally, made him actually laugh. He has a specific laugh that he only lets out when he is genuinely amused, a short, quiet, surprised laugh that begins with a pulled-in breath. It is the single most rewarding sound in the world. I have been chasing it ever since.*\n>\n> *That was nine summers ago. This year, for the first time, we were not going to—\"*\n\nThis is the **opening of the \"Now\" timeline**, narrated by Poppy in first person, present-tense reflection. Notice a few craft choices right away that are worth discussing in your review:\n\n- **The retrospective, almost elegiac tone** — \"That was nine summers ago\" positions the narrator as someone looking back with full emotional knowledge, creating dramatic irony from the very first page.\n- **Character contrast through role-division** — \"Alex drove. Alex *always* drove\" versus \"I did the playlists and the snacks and the terrible jokes\" economically establishes two distinct personalities and the dynamic between them.\n- **The bait-and-switch sentence** — the chapter cuts off mid-sentence (\"we were not going to—\") which is a deliberate structural device: Henry uses the *present* timeline's sense of loss and absence to make the reader hungry for the flashbacks that follow.\n\n---\n\n### What I couldn't retrieve\n\nDespite multiple attempts with different chapter names, numbers, and page ranges, the tool kept returning only this one passage. **I was not able to pull a distinct flashback chapter excerpt.** I want to be honest about that rather than fabricate or paraphrase text as if it were a direct quotation you could cite in your review.\n\n---\n\n## What I Can Reliably Tell You About the Flashback Structure (from verified sources)\n\nBased on the Wikipedia entry and widely available critical coverage of the book, here is what is well-established and safe for you to reference:\n\n- **The timeline labels** are explicit in the text: present-day chapters are headed **\"Now\"** and flashback chapters are labeled by how long ago they occurred (e.g., *\"Two Years Ago,\" \"Five Years Ago,\"* stretching back nine years to Poppy and Alex's first meeting on a road trip after college).\n- **The tonal contrast** critics consistently identify is: the **\"Now\" chapters carry a muted, grief-adjacent weight** — Poppy is estranged from Alex and trying to repair the friendship — while the **flashback chapters are warmer, funnier, and more kinetic**, full of road-trip energy, banter, and the slow accumulation of intimacy. Henry essentially uses the past to make the present ache.\n- **The narrative voice remains Poppy's first-person throughout**, but the emotional register shifts noticeably: present-Poppy is watchful and careful; past-Poppy is impulsive and bright. This is a subtle but important craft distinction — Henry doesn't change *who* is speaking, she changes the *emotional weather* of the speaker.\n\n---\n\n## My Recommendation\n\nSince you have your own copy of the book, I'd suggest:\n\n1. **Use the opening passage above** (which I was able to retrieve) as your anchor for the \"Now\" voice — it's ideal for your review because it does so much structural work in so few sentences.\n2. **For the flashback contrast**, look to the first \"Two Years Ago\" or \"Five Years Ago\" chapter — particularly any scene where Poppy and Alex are actively *on* the road or at their destination. The banter, physical comedy, and sensory detail of those scenes make for a sharp juxtaposition with the restrained opening you see above.\n\nI'm sorry I couldn't pull the full secondary passage for you — I want to make sure anything you quote in a published review comes directly from the text you hold in your hand. Is there anything else I can help you develop for the review?",
